  "body": "Closes #309.\n\nErdős 80: with $f_c(n)$ the largest book forced on every $n$-vertex graph with at least $cn^2$ edges whose every edge lies in a triangle, estimate $f_c(n)$; in particular is $f_c(n) > n^\\epsilon$, or $f_c(n) \\gg \\log n$?\n\nA book is `#(G.trianglesContaining e)`, using the `trianglesContaining` already in `ForMathlib/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/Clique.lean`, so nothing new was needed for it:\n\n```lean\nnoncomputable def bookNumber : ℕ :=\n  G.edgeFinset.sup fun e => #(G.trianglesContaining e)\n```\n\n`f c n` is `sInf` of the book numbers over admissible graphs, which is what \"the maximal `m` that every such graph must contain\" comes to. Both questions in the problem are stated, the $n^\\epsilon$ one as the headline and $\\gg \\log n$ as a variant, since the source asks both.\n\n`sInf ∅ = 0`, so `f c n = 0` both when some admissible graph has no book and when no graph on `n` vertices has $cn^2$ edges at all. The second is the honest reading, nothing is forced when nothing qualifies, and it only bites for `c` large enough that the class is empty, which the asymptotic questions do not reach.\n\nThree statements are proved rather than `sorry`: that `bookNumber` vanishes exactly when no edge lies in a triangle, and the two `⊥` cases. `#print axioms` on each gives only `propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`.\n\n`lake --wfail build` is clean.\n\n",
